First real model measured against ai/eval/harness - PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md
§6.4's actual gate ("سجّل... citation precision، Top-1/Top-3 cause coverage"
per candidate). Everything before this log (docs/evidence/16, 17) built the
yardstick; this is the first real number measured with it.

## Candidate chosen, and why it differs from the plan's own §6.1 hypothesis

§6.1 named Qwen3-4B Q4_K_M as the starting hypothesis. Checked before
downloading anything: Qwen2.5's 3B-parameter GGUF releases ship under the
"Qwen Research License" - non-commercial only - which would be a real,
avoidable licensing blocker for a product whose licensing model is already
an open business question (docs/product/licensing-plan.md). Chose
microsoft/Phi-4-mini-instruct instead: MIT-licensed (confirmed directly from
its HuggingFace repo's LICENSE file), 3.8B parameters, comparable size class
to the plan's own hypothesis. This is a deliberate, reasoned substitution,
not a silent deviation - the plan's own candidate remains worth benchmarking
later if a permissively-licensed Qwen release becomes relevant.

Downloaded, with the operator's explicit approval (stated filename/source/
size beforehand, per this session's own file-download policy):
- `bartowski/microsoft_Phi-4-mini-instruct-GGUF`, `*-Q4_K_M.gguf`, 2,491,874,688
  bytes. Verified after download: real GGUF v3 file, 196 tensors, architecture
  "phi3", name "Phi 4 Mini Instruct" (not a corrupted/partial download).
- `ggml-org/llama.cpp` release `b10948`, `llama-b10948-bin-win-cpu-x64.zip`
  (CPU-only build, no GPU/driver dependency needed for this bounded eval) -
  MIT-licensed, official upstream GitHub release, fetched via `gh release
  download` (exact asset name/size confirmed via `gh api` before downloading,
  not guessed).

Both live under `ai/eval/models/`, gitignored (multi-GB binaries, never
versioned - this log is the permanent record of what was fetched and from
where).

## Two real bugs found and fixed before trusting any number

**Bug 1 - llama-cli + JSON-schema grammar + jinja chat template**: a direct
smoke test (`llama-cli -jf schema.json -sys ... -p ...`) failed outright:
`Error: Failed to initialize samplers: Unexpected empty grammar stack after
accepting piece: assistant`. Isolated by testing with and without `--no-jinja`
- the jinja-templated chat-role text interacts badly with schema-grammar
sampling in this llama.cpp build (b10948). Fixed by adding `--no-jinja`
(llama.cpp's built-in, non-jinja Phi-3/Phi-4 template still applies correct
role formatting - confirmed the model answered a trivial smoke-test question
correctly, in valid schema JSON, with this flag).

**Bug 2 - a real bug in this repo's own `ai/eval/run` extraction code, not
the model**: the first full benchmark run (results discarded, not this run)
scored 1/4 pass with 3 of 4 cases showing "no hypothesis offered" - despite
the raw llama-cli transcript, read by hand, clearly showing the model HAD
produced full, substantive JSON answers. Root cause:
`extractModelOutput`'s first version used `strings.LastIndex(raw, "{")` to
find the model's answer inside llama-cli's full terminal transcript; because
the model's own answer JSON contains NESTED objects (one per
`ranked_hypotheses` entry), the textually-last `{` in the whole transcript
was an INNER brace belonging to one hypothesis object, not the outer answer
object - forward-matching from there silently extracted just
`{"cause":...,"entity":...,"confidence":...}` instead of the real answer.
That small object unmarshals into `harness.ModelOutput` without error (Go
ignores unrecognised fields), producing an all-zero-value result that the
harness correctly, but meaninglessly, graded as "no hypothesis offered."

Fixed the scanning logic (track brace depth, keep the LAST complete span
that both opens and closes at depth 0), added 3 regression tests built
directly from this failure shape (`ai/eval/run/main_test.go`). Re-ran with
the fix - **still wrong**, and for a genuinely different, more interesting
reason: llama-cli's own interactive terminal ECHO of a long input prompt
truncates a string value mid-display for readability (inserting literal
"... (truncated)" text into what is supposed to be an exact JSON echo).
That truncation permanently unbalances any brace-depth counter trying to
re-parse the transcript from the start - the model's real answer JSON later
in the transcript is itself perfectly well-formed, but the counter never
gets back to a clean depth-0 baseline to recognise it, because the echoed
prompt before it is now corrupted mid-stream. Confirmed by feeding the exact
captured transcript into a standalone test: the scanner found exactly ONE
complete top-level span (the very first echoed event object) and nothing
after it in the entire remaining text, matching the truncation's position.

This is a structural problem with re-parsing llama-cli's interactive-mode
terminal output at all, not something a smarter regex fixes. **Switched the
runner from shelling out to `llama-cli` per case to calling `llama-server`'s
OpenAI-compatible `/v1/chat/completions` HTTP endpoint instead** - the
response body IS the model's answer with no terminal-display layer in
between to reintroduce this class of bug. `ai/eval/run/main.go`'s package
doc comment carries the full reasoning. The existing brace-depth extractor
is kept (still useful for stripping an occasional markdown code fence around
the JSON) but now only ever sees a single, clean response body, not a mixed
transcript.

## The real, trustworthy result (dev split, 4 non-deidentified cases)

Command:
```
llama-server -m ai/eval/models/Phi-4-mini-instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf \
  --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8811 -t 8 --no-jinja -jf schema.json &
go run ./ai/eval/run -split dev -server http://127.0.0.1:8811 -n-predict 700
```

```
[collector_down-ambiguous-en] FAIL - offered a confident hypothesis for a case where refusal was the correct answer
[gateway_hijack-positive-en]  FAIL - cited event id not present in the input: 01M2DNG9B46EKK7Q83SZQ9PKV6; confidence exceeds evidence's own ceiling (x2)
[normal_traffic-negative-en]  PASS - correct refusal
[rogue_dhcp-positive-en]      PASS - Top-1 hit, Top-3 hit, citation precision 0.50

pass (no hard violations): 2/4
top-1 cause hit: 1/4
top-3 cause hit: 1/4
correct refusals: 1/1 (the one true-negative case)
mean citation precision (non-empty citations): 0.17
```

Full raw model output for all 4 cases: `ai/eval/results/20260913T224921Z/results.json`
(gitignored - regenerable by re-running against the same model; this log is
the permanent record of what it said).

## Reading the two real failures honestly, not just the pass/fail label

**gateway_hijack's fabricated citation is a genuinely interesting, specific
mistake**, not random noise: the real ARP-change event's `event_id` is
`01M2DNG9B46EKK7Q83SYPGC6KY`, but that SAME event's `subject.id` (an internal
host identifier, not an event ID) is `01M2DNG9B46EKK7Q83SZQ9PKV6` - the two
ULIDs share a long timestamp-derived prefix and differ only in their last
few characters (`...SYPGC6KY` vs `...SZQ9PKV6`), because ULIDs generated
moments apart naturally share that prefix. The model appears to have
confused the subject's internal ID with the event's own ID, and used the
wrong one as both its `evidence_event_ids` citation and its `entity` value
(where the expected entity was the human-readable `"10.99.0.201"`). This is
exactly the class of error the harness's fabrication check exists to catch
- caught correctly this time, on a real, non-obvious mistake, not an
artifact of this session's own tooling.

**collector_down's over-confident hypothesis is a real limitation, not a
harness quirk**: the model correctly identified that collectors were down
and cited that fact - accurately - as its summary, but the case's grading
requires refusal here specifically because "the collector was down" is a
signal that the underlying connectivity question CANNOT be answered, not
itself a satisfying answer to it (matching the "recorder-was-blind"/
"collector-not-watching" rules' own semantics, §6.2: "ارفض الاستنتاج في
gap/collector-down"). A 3.8B model at Q4 quantization did not make this
distinction. Whether a larger model, a different prompt emphasising this
distinction more explicitly, or few-shot examples would fix this is an open
question for a later candidate/prompt iteration - recorded honestly as a
real gap, not smoothed over.

## Scope of this first run, stated plainly

- Only 4 of the dev split's 6 cases were run: the two `-deidentified`
  variants (`gateway_hijack-positive-en-deidentified`,
  `rogue_dhcp-positive-en-deidentified`) were deliberately skipped
  (`-skip-deidentified` defaults true) because this runner does not yet
  replicate `ai/eval/gen`'s address-substitution table at eval time - feeding
  real addresses as event context while the question itself was de-identified
  would defeat the point of that case variant. Real, stated limitation, not
  hidden; fixing it is a small, well-scoped follow-up (compute the same
  first-seen-order `<HOST_N>` substitution the generator uses, applied to the
  loaded event context before building the prompt).
- Only the `dev` split was run, per the plan's own methodology (`test` stays
  held out until a real model-selection decision is being made, not spent on
  every exploratory run).
- Only English questions were used - Arabic-language benchmarking of the
  same cases has not been attempted yet.
- One model, one quantization, one prompt. This is not yet a comparison
  across candidates/quantizations/prompts as §6.4's fuller benchmark table
  calls for - it is the first real number, proving the whole pipeline
  (download → serve → prompt → parse → grade) works end-to-end against a
  real model, honestly scored.
- CPU-only inference, 8 threads: 42 seconds to 2m40s per case depending on
  event-context size. Not fast enough for interactive use in this
  configuration - expected and unsurprising for a 3.8B Q4 model on CPU, and
  irrelevant to whether the SCORES are trustworthy, which is what this run
  was actually testing.

## Verification

- `gofmt -l`, `go vet ./ai/eval/...`: clean.
- `go test ./ai/eval/run/... -v`: 3/3 new regression tests pass (built from
  the actual bug-2 failure shape), plus the pre-existing harness/gen suites
  unaffected.
- `go build ./ai/eval/...`: succeeds.
- Manually cross-checked 2 of the 4 raw model answers against the real
  corpus event data (`corpus/v1/gateway_hijack/events.json`,
  `corpus/v1/rogue_dhcp/events.json`) to confirm the harness's violations
  and hits are real, not artifacts - see the "reading the failures honestly"
  section above.
- `curl http://127.0.0.1:8811/health` before and during the run: server
  stayed up throughout; no crashes.
- `llama-server` stopped cleanly after this run (not left running).

